<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Lesser Armenia</h1><p class="st-meta">331 BCE – 72 CE (approx.) · capital Nicopolis</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Lesser Armenia, a highland kingdom on the upper Euphrates that passed between Pontus, Galatian chiefs and Roman client kings.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After Alexander's conquest, the mountainous land west of the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> was ruled by local Armenian dynasts, separate from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-armenia-orontid">Kingdom of Armenia (Orontid)</a>. In the late 2nd century BCE its ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antipater">Antipater</a> ceded it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-pontus">Kingdom of Pontus</a>, who built many fortresses to store his treasures.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Lesser <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/armenia">Armenia</a> was a land of pastures and fortified hilltops. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates</a> in 66 BCE, he founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicopolis-lesser-armenia">Nicopolis</a> on the battlefield and gave the region to the Galatian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deiotarus">Deiotarus</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> later used it as a reward for loyal client kings.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Its last king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristobulus-of-chalcis">Aristobulus</a>, a great-grandson of Herod, ruled until c. 71 CE. Vespasian annexed Lesser <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/armenia">Armenia</a> to the Roman province of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-cappadocia">Cappadocia</a> in 72 CE.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lesser-armenia.jpg" alt="Lesser Armenia" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Samhanin · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Rubenid_Dynasty.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>331 BCE – 72 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nicopolis</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Antipater, Deiotarus, Aristobulus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zoroastrianism, Anatolian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Armenian, Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Armenia Minor, Kingdom of Lesser Armenia, Lesser Armenia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-130&amp;focus=lesser-armenia" aria-label="Lesser Armenia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5570 845 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lesser Armenia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5890 1101L5879 982M5890 1101L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5879 982h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5890 1101h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5890 1101h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-130&amp;focus=lesser-armenia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
